      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}8 x y + 4 y^{2}&=- 4 x^{2} + 3 y - 9\\\\4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 9&=0\\end{aligned}$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 9$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$\n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$t(x,y)=f_y(x,y)\\,i-f_x(x,y)\\,j$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)&=\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 9)\\,i-\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(4 x^{2} + 8 x y + 4 y^{2} - 3 y + 9)\\,j\\\\&=(8 x + 8 y - 3)\\,i-(8 x + 8 y)\\,j\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $j$, we must have $t\\cdot j=0.$\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot j&=0\\\\- 8 x - 8 y&=0\\\\y&=-x\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSubstituting $y=-x$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}4(x+(-x))^{2}-3(-x)-(-9)&=0\\\\3x-(-9)&=0\\\\3x&=-9\\\\x&=-3\\end{aligned}$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-3,3)$."
